WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:04.910
- This is some of the stock that we've got

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:07.010
for the Chinese market.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:08.840
- [Narrator] Mitchell Taylor's
winery has been selling

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.900
Australian wine since the 1960s.

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.610
- It retails for approximately
a thousand dollars a bottle.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:17.070
- [Narrator] And in the past five years,

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.181
China has been his main focus.

00:00:19.181 --> 00:00:22.367
- That's the back label there
with all the Chinese details.

00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:24.720
- [Narrator] But these
bottles can now be three times

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.770
as expensive, because China
has imposed new tariffs

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:31.011
of up to 212% on Australian wine.

00:00:31.011 --> 00:00:35.220
- I was absolutely shocked,
it really did hit me hard.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.940
- [Narrator] Since May,
Australia has been caught

00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:38.630
in a tree dispute with China.

00:00:38.630 --> 00:00:40.280
It isn't not just the wine industry,

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:43.930
but also coal, barley, lobster, and beef.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:47.660
Some economists say as much
as 6% of Australia's GDP

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:48.933
hangs in the balance.

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:53.200
- It's an attack on free
countries everywhere.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.610
- [Narrator] The bilateral
trade dispute has gone global.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:58.530
China hawks and lawmakers from
across the political spectrum

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:01.700
and around the world, are
taking Australia's side.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:04.400
The White House National
Security Council even tweeted

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.970
that it would serve Australian wine

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.200
at its holiday reception.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.950
Politicians are jumping on
China's wine tariff to draw

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:14.270
a global attention to what
they see as Beijing's bullying.

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:17.260
And Australia's winemakers hope
this new coalition can help

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:18.820
save their industry.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:20.010
- It's a great initiative.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:23.250
The coalition of partners
can come together

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:27.343
and help us all put pressure
on the Chinese regime.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.080
- The virus began in China in Wuhan,

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:34.220
that's I think well understood.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:36.370
- [Narrator] This statement
is what sparked the dispute,

00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:38.860
when back in April, the
Australian government called

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:40.710
for an investigation into the origin

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:42.443
of the coronavirus pandemic.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:45.450
Beijing, which has suggested
that the virus came

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.798
from outside China,
dispelled the criticism.

00:01:48.798 --> 00:01:52.548
(speaks in foreign language)

00:01:57.799 --> 00:01:59.070
- [Narrator] But this diplomatic tussle

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.770
is about more than the virus.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:04.040
China has pointed out several
grievances against Australia,

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:05.840
like when it banned Chinese telecom

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.270
from Huawei from its 5G network,

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:10.680
because it said to posed a
national security threat,

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:13.070
even though Huawei said
it has operated safely

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:14.283
in the country.

00:02:14.283 --> 00:02:15.700
(crowd chanting)

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:17.320
And when Australia condemned China's

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:20.130
controversial national
security law in Hong Kong,

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:23.020
which Beijing said is a domestic matter.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.700
So in May, China slapped
tariffs of up to 80% on barley,

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.960
followed by levies on other goods.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.083
Timber exports were also
stopped at Chinese customs.

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:35.550
For entrepreneurs like Mitchell Taylor,

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.830
these tensions threaten their businesses.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:40.960
A safe bet big on China, and
invested to expand production

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.260
by buying new land and
upgrading facilities.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:46.980
- Our current sales
into the Chinese market

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:49.010
are about 10 to 15%.

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:52.410
We've been explaining our
wines and matching them

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.690
with the very good Chinese cuisine.

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:57.780
- [Narrator] Taylor says
sales in China have doubled

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.540
over the past four years,
as drinking wine became

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.023
a status symbol for the
Chinese middle-class.

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.490
To appeal to more Chinese drinkers,

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:09.260
Taylor adapted his product.

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.580
- The Chinese customers, they're
fascinated with technology.

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:18.580
We have a microchip here that
is a great security seal.

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:20.810
- Taylor also uses gold on labels,

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:23.390
which is perceived as
a lucky color in China.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:25.690
He created heavy wooden boxes that he said

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:28.483
Chinese customers
associate with exclusivity,

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:32.220
and the company even produced
wines that Taylor says

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:34.623
Chinese consumers like
to have with hotpot.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.050
Taylor's isn't the only
winery in Australia

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:40.140
to have bet big on China.

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.900
Exports there have tripled
in the past five years,

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.323
to hit $907 million in 2019.

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:51.010
Australia's economy as a whole
has come to rely on China,

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:54.190
sending a third of its
exports to the mainland.

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:57.500
- Australia has not had
an economic recession

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:59.200
in about two decades,

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.770
and much of that is
thanks to China's rise.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:06.120
- [Narrator] Vicky Xu is an
analyst who's been studying

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.520
Australia's increasingly
tense relationship with China.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.620
Despite the closer economic relationship

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.080
between both countries,

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.400
Australia's government
has been raising its voice

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.750
on political issues sensitive to Beijing.

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:20.050
- People in the business
community are very anxious

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:21.020
here in Australia.

00:04:21.020 --> 00:04:23.560
And that the current
administration is speaking out

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:26.000
assertively on issues like human rights.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:28.220
But the question is,
can we really stand up

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:32.100
for our values despite economic losses?

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.400
- We will not be bullied.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.360
- This trade dispute,
especially China's wine tariff,

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.460
has caught the attention of a new alliance

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:41.000
of more than a hundred lawmakers

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:43.100
from more than a dozen countries.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:45.330
They say trade policies shouldn't be used

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:47.210
as a political tool.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:49.770
- We are asking you all
to join us in standing

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:52.890
against Xi Jinping's
authoritarian bullying.

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:56.210
- By drinking a bottle or
two of Australian wine.

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:57.640
- [Narrator] As trade disputes multiply

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:01.100
in a globalized economy, Vicky
Xu says countries that share

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:03.430
political values could increasingly rely

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:04.633
on each other's market.

00:05:05.758 --> 00:05:07.830
- [Vicky Xu] Everyone
knows they will have help,

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:11.560
and they will have backup when
they're also being punished

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:14.773
by Beijing for perceived indiscretion.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.970
- We really do appreciate our allies

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:19.660
and friends coming together.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:24.237
We've got an opportunity now
to relaunch in the US market,

00:05:24.237 --> 00:05:26.523
the UK and in Canada.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:30.190
- [Narrator] But because
consumers there are different

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:32.790
from the Chinese, Taylor
is tweaking the marketing

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:35.385
around his lineup of more than 50 bottles.

00:05:35.385 --> 00:05:39.070
- The labels in Chinese,
we'll have to rip them off.

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:42.270
We'll have to rebottle
it, and then repackage it.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.370
- [Narrator] For the US market,

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:45.640
Taylor is using floral labels to appeal

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:47.530
to young urban consumers.

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:49.390
And will start exporting vegan wine

00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:51.803
to cities like New York and Los Angeles.

00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:54.770
And for the longterm,

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:56.690
he's planting more Shiraz and Grenache,

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:58.973
which are in popularity in the West.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:03.593
Still, it will be difficult
to make up for lost business.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:07.630
Some Australia-based
foreign policy analysts hope

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:09.840
with the incoming Biden administration,

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:12.970
like-minded countries could
work together to confront China,

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:15.100
as the president-elect
has said that he will seek

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.430
to strengthen global alliances.

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:19.340
- The idea that we're
gonna be able to deal

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:21.550
with China's rogue behavior,

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:24.530
when we make up 25% of
the world's economy.

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:27.160
Without bringing in the
other 40% of our allies,

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:28.453
is not realistic.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:31.980
- [Narrator] Meanwhile,
China says it's committed

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:34.860
to free trade and that it's
tariff measures are in line

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.020
with international customary practices.

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:38.800
And that it's now up to Australia

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:40.323
to repair the relationship.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:44.480
Taylor knows he's closing out a tough 2020

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:47.350
without the same access
to the China market.

00:06:47.350 --> 00:06:50.115
But he says he's learned
a lesson for next year.

00:06:50.115 --> 00:06:53.880
- This whole incident has really taught us

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:56.480
not to get too focused on one customer,

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:58.653
you've just gotta diversify and do that.

